 "attribution": "Elevation: Copernicus DEM GLO-30 (© DLR e.V. 2010–2014 / © Airbus Defence and Space GmbH, ESA), bare-earth filtered · Terrain shading: no aerial imagery (hypsometric tint) · Roads © OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL) · Buildings © OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL)",
 "scenario": {
  "description": "Chungthang stands where the Lachen Chu and the Lachung Chu meet to form the Teesta, and the 60 m dam of the 1,200 MW Teesta-III hydropower project stood just below it. At 10:13 pm on 3 October 2023 about 14.7 million m³ of frozen moraine collapsed into South Lhonak Lake, 5,200 m up and some 68 km upstream; the wave it raised, about 20 m high, breached the moraine dam, and about 50 million m³ of water — half the lake — came down the valley. It reached Chungthang around 00:30, about two and a quarter hours later, and destroyed the dam (Sattar et al., Science, 2025). The flood entering this map is modelled, not measured: a 1-D hazard-chain model raises the wave, breaches the dam (59 million m³ released, against about 50 reported) and routes the outburst 63 km down the valley; this is the median of its 27 runs, peaking at about 9,578 m³/s at the north edge. No gauge survived here, and the published peaks at Chungthang are reconstructions that disagree by a factor of two (7,355 to 14,673 m³/s); the model’s runs span 4,133–15,318 m³/s at Chungthang. The clock is the event's: the run starts at 23:48, five minutes before the modelled front reaches the north edge, and the model has the front at Chungthang at 00:01 (its runs span 23:52–00:48) against about 00:30 observed — earlier than the real flood, and that gap is the model’s error. The real flood was a debris flood that moved hundreds of millions of cubic metres of sediment, and inside the map the solver caps its speed (15 m/s unless the debris-flood setting raises the cap to 25). The terrain is Copernicus 30 m radar data, a surface model with canopy and buildings in it, acquired in 2011–2015 while the dam was being built, so neither the dam nor its reservoir is in it. The bare-earth filter only runs on ground flatter than 20°, and 94 % of this domain is steeper: the valley floor is filtered and still a metre or two high, the walls above it are the raw surface model. No photograph is baked in — open satellite imagery of this valley could be redistributed, but composing a cloud-free mosaic of it was beyond this bake — so the ground is shaded by height instead. OpenStreetMap maps only the highway and a few lanes here, and one named place on the road network stands clear of the river. The rivers start dry and get five minutes of the modelled baseflow before the front, so almost every drop you see is the modelled flood. Watch which ground goes under and in what order, and do not read street-level depths.",
